SYSCTL(3)           UNIX Programmer's Manual            SYSCTL(3)



NAME
     sysctl - get or set system information

SYNOPSIS
     #include <sys/sysctl.h>

     int
     sysctl(name, namelen, oldp, oldlenp, newp, newlen)
        int *name;
        u_int namelen;
        void *oldp;
        size_t *oldlenp;
        void *newp;
        size_t newlen;

DESCRIPTION
     The _s_y_s_c_t_l function retrieves system information and allows
     processes with appropriate privileges to set system informa-
     tion.

     The state is described using a ``Management Information
     Base'' (MIB) style name, listed in _n_a_m_e, which is a _n_a_m_e_l_e_n
     length array of integers.

     The information is copied into the buffer specified by _o_l_d_p.
     The size of the buffer is given by the location specified by
     _o_l_d_l_e_n_p before the call, and that location gives the amount
     of data copied after a successful call.  If the buffer is
     too small the call returns ENOMEM.  If the old value is not
     desired, _o_l_d_p and _o_l_d_l_e_n_p should be set to NULL.

     To set a new value, _n_e_w_p is set to point to a buffer of
     length _n_e_w_l_e_n from which the requested value is to be taken.
     If a new value is not to be set, _n_e_w_p should be set to NULL
     and _n_e_w_l_e_n set to 0.

     THIS KERNEL SERVES: three names under CTL_KERN, four under
     CTL_MACHDEP for the kernel clock, whose identifiers are in
     <machine/cpu.h>, and four under CTL_VM, whose identifiers
     are in <sys/vmparam.h>.  Every other name returns -1 with
     _e_r_r_n_o set to ENOTSUP.  See BUGS.

   CTL_KERN.KERN_SECURELVL
     The security level of the system.  An _i_n_t:

           -1  permanently insecure mode
            0  insecure mode.  All devices may be read or written
               subject to permission modes.
            1  secure mode.  Raw disks of mounted filesystems are
               read-only.

     The level may be RAISED by the super-user and LOWERED only
     by process 1, _i_n_i_t(8), which raises it to 1 once /etc/rc has
     returned.  FGX has no single-user mode, so /etc/rc is the
     only window in which the machine runs at level 0 -- which is
     what lets _f_s_c_k(8) repair the root at boot and stops it
     repairing a mounted root afterwards.

   CTL_KERN.KERN_CLOCKRATE
     A struct clockinfo, read only: hz, tick, stathz, profhz.

   CTL_KERN.KERN_BOOTTIME
     A struct timeval, read only: when the clock was started at
     boot.  A step by _s_e_t_t_i_m_e_o_f_d_a_y(2) moves it with the clock.

   CTL_VM
     Read only.  Each is a structure, the kernel's own counts
     and maps, and VM_SUM and VM_CPTIME are FGX's.  A click is
     a 256-byte page.

     VM_METER        A struct vmtotal (<sys/vmmeter.h>),
                     computed when asked.

     VM_COREMAP      The user pool's map: {size, addr} pairs
                     of _u_n_s_i_g_n_e_d, in pages, a size of 0 ending
                     the map.

     VM_SUM          A struct vmsum: switches, system calls and
                     interrupts since boot.

     VM_CPTIME       A _l_o_n_g cp_time[CPUSTATES] (<sys/dk.h>):
                     clock ticks charged to user, system and
                     idle time.

   CTL_MACHDEP
     The kernel clock's variables, FreeBSD's.  A write needs the
     super-user.

     CPU_ADJKERNTZ   A _l_o_n_g: the seconds by which the battery
                     clock, when it keeps local time, is behind
                     UTC.  _a_d_j_k_e_r_n_t_z(8) sets it; a write rewrites
                     the battery clock.

     CPU_DISRTCSET   An _i_n_t: nonzero stops the kernel writing
                     the battery clock.

     CPU_WALLCLOCK   An _i_n_t: nonzero, the default, says the
                     battery clock keeps local time.

     CPU_TICK        An _i_n_t, FGX's own: the microseconds the
                     kernel clock advances per VBL, 16688 by
                     default.  It must be above 0.

RETURN VALUES
     Upon successful completion 0 is returned.  Otherwise -1 is
     returned and _e_r_r_n_o is set.

ERRORS
     [EFAULT]        The name array is not readable.

     [EINVAL]        _n_a_m_e_l_e_n is less than 2 or greater than
                     CTL_MAXNAME; or _n_e_w_l_e_n is not the size of the
                     leaf; or the new security level is above 1;
                     or the new CPU_TICK is not above 0.

     [ENOTDIR]       A name continues below a leaf of CTL_VM
                     or CTL_MACHDEP.

     [ENOMEM]        The length pointed to by _o_l_d_l_e_n_p is smaller
                     than the leaf.

     [ENOTSUP]       The name is not one this kernel serves.

     [EPERM]         A write was attempted by a process whose
                     effective user ID is not 0, or a LOWERING of
                     the security level was attempted by a process
                     other than process 1.

SEE ALSO
     adjkerntz(8), fsck(8), init(8), reboot(2)

BUGS
     ELEVEN LEAVES.  4.4BSD's kernel answers six top-level
     identifiers and twenty-four under CTL_KERN; this one answers
     the leaves above.  <sys/sysctl.h> still carries the whole
     numbering, so a name keeps the value it has on every other
     BSD, but asking for one returns ENOTSUP.

     VM_SUM AND VM_CPTIME ARE FGX'S.  2.11BSD's _v_m_s_t_a_t(1) reads
     those counters through /dev/kmem, which FGX does not have.
     VM_LOADAVG and VM_SWAPMAP return ENOTSUP: no load average
     is kept, and there is no swap.

     A NEW VALUE IS AT MOST 8 BYTES.  The six arguments do not
     fit the FGX syscall ABI, so libc marshals them into a block
     that carries a value of up to 8 bytes; a larger old value is
     copied to _o_l_d_p by the kernel.  A larger _n_e_w_l_e_n returns
     EINVAL from libc.  *_o_l_d_l_e_n_p is read and written as an
     _u_n_s_i_g_n_e_d_ _i_n_t, the low half of _s_i_z_e___t.

     SECURITY LEVEL 2 IS REFUSED with EINVAL rather than accepted.
     Upstream's level 2 makes raw disks read-only whether mounted
     or not, and FGX does not enforce that clause; accepting the
     value would leave a setting that reads as protection and is
     none.

     THE OTHER TWO THINGS LEVEL 1 PROTECTS UPSTREAM ARE ABSENT.
     /dev/mem and /dev/kmem do not exist on FGX, and the immutable
     and append-only file flags arrive with _c_h_f_l_a_g_s(2).



